==English==
{{wikipedia|Extraversion and introversion}}
===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|extravert}} {{q|proper}}

===Etymology===
A variant of {{m|en|extravert}}, popularized in psychology by Phyllis Blanchard's 1918 "Psycho-Analytic Study of August Comte", equivalent to {{prefix|en|extro-|vert}}.

===Pronunciation 1===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈɛkstrəvəːt/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈɛkstrəˌvərt/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-extrovert.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɜː(ɹ)t|lang=en}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|informal|_|psychology}} An [[extroverted]] [[person]]: one who is [[outgoing]], [[sociable]], and [[concern]]ed with [[outer]] [[affairs]].
#* '''1918''' April, Phyllis Blanchard, "A Psycho-Analytic Study of August Comte", ''American Journal of Psychology'', p. 163:
#*: In order to understand the marked contract between Comte's mental attitude during his early years and that of his later life, we must keep in mind [[:w:Carl Jung|Jung]]'s hypothesis of the two psychological types, the introvert and '''extrovert''',—the thinking type and the feeling type.

=====Usage notes=====
Technical papers in psychology overwhelmingly prefer {{m|en|extravert}}, the variant used by [[:w:Carl Jung|Carl Jung]], although the spelling ''extrovert'' has become more common in general use.

=====Antonyms=====
* {{l|en|introvert}}

